00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:03.779
Sophie Bulloch: Okay, so, there are some source code changes that we have been successful at creating good first issue.

364
00:43:04.080 --> 00:43:12.309
Sophie Bulloch: That's usually extremely specific. So, for example, something also generic that is… isn't DLD-specific.

365
00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:19.520
Sophie Bulloch: So, for example, it would be, can you create a way of printing the information in this file?

366
00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:21.269
Sophie Bulloch: In a prettier way.

367
00:43:21.390 --> 00:43:23.319
Sophie Bulloch: So, creating a string function.

368
00:43:24.150 --> 00:43:26.129
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): But we still have to say.

369
00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:31.839
Sophie Bulloch: In general, what that will mean, because maybe we have some specific

370
00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:44.459
Sophie Bulloch: classes or specific ways of coding it that isn't necessarily generic and is kind of like this. We will also tend to have to guide them with how to do the test.

371
00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:51.810
Sophie Bulloch: So, for example, what we expect from a good first issue is something that's really documented.

372
00:43:52.000 --> 00:43:57.300
Sophie Bulloch: So we actually say, like, okay, if we are going to suggest a new string function.

373
00:43:57.570 --> 00:44:00.880
Sophie Bulloch: We tell them roughly, what to do.

374
00:44:03.100 --> 00:44:15.569
Sophie Bulloch: we won't give them the answer, but it will roughly tell them exactly what they need to do. And in the proposed solution, we will tell them, okay, this is what we want you to do, we would want you to create a function here.

375
00:44:15.740 --> 00:44:23.060
Sophie Bulloch: And if we have to test it, then we would say, we need this test, we need this test, we need this test, it needs to do this, it needs to do this, so the good first issue.

376
00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:30.059
Sophie Bulloch: Would be probably more of an investment, for the source code things, but that's… that's something that has worked before, because it's so…

377
00:44:30.750 --> 00:44:36.639
Sophie Bulloch: it's so specific. In terms of things that have not been successful.

378
00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:43.650
Sophie Bulloch: We have recently… we pushed a bot, and this has unassigned a few people because they were stale.

379
00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:46.000
Sophie Bulloch: So we do have some examples.

